select m.id, 2, $L$POST & Creating Resources$L$, $L$14 minute read$L$, $L$`POST` creates new resources and, unlike `GET`, it changes server state. Testing it well means checking what got created, not just that the request returned.$L$, array[$L$Explain what a `POST` request does and why it is not safe$L$, $L$Send a `POST` with a JSON body and correct headers$L$, $L$Verify a creation using status `201` and the response body$L$], $L$## What POST does
`POST` **creates** a new resource in a collection. It is **not safe** (it changes state) and **not idempotent** — send the same `POST` twice and you usually get two resources.

## The request body
`POST` carries a **body**, almost always JSON. You must declare it:
- Header `Content-Type: application/json`
- Body `{"name":"Ada","role":"engineer"}`

## The successful response
- Status `201 Created` (not just `200`).
- A `Location` header pointing at the new resource, e.g. `/users/99`.
- A body echoing the created resource, now with a server-assigned `id`.

## What testers check
- Status is `201` and a new `id` was assigned.
- The returned fields **match what you sent**.
- Invalid input is rejected with `400 Bad Request`, not silently accepted.
- A duplicate create behaves as designed (`409 Conflict` if uniqueness is enforced).$L$, $L$Request: `POST https://reqres.in/api/users` with `Content-Type: application/json` and body `{"name":"Ada","job":"engineer"}`. Response: `201 Created` with body `{"name":"Ada","job":"engineer","id":"482","createdAt":"..."}`. Checks: status is `201`, an `id` appeared, and the `name`/`job` echo the input exactly.$L$, $L$- Forgetting `Content-Type: application/json`, so the body is ignored or rejected
- Accepting `200` when the contract specifies `201 Created`
- Only checking the status and never confirming the resource was actually created$L$, $L$After a `POST`, always do a follow-up `GET` on the new resource's id. A `201` means "I accepted it" — the `GET` proves it was actually stored the way you expected.$L$, $L$In Postman, `POST` to `https://reqres.in/api/users` with a JSON body of your choice. Confirm you get `201`, note the assigned `id`, and check the response echoes your fields.$L$, $L$Why is `POST` not idempotent, and when could that cause a real problem?$L$, $L$What status code should a successful resource creation return? (Answer: 201 Created)$L$, array[$L$I can send a `POST` with a JSON body and correct headers$L$, $L$I can verify a `201` and the assigned `id`$L$, $L$I can confirm the created resource matches my input$L$]
